#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 4.6.22
# DESCRIPTION
# Given a position function s(t), find:
#    (a) The velocity and acceleration functions
#    (b) Position, velocity, speed, & acceleration at t = B
#    (c) The times when the particle is stopped
#    (d) The times when the particle is speeding up/slowing down

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Rates of change - engineering and physics)
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## MLT(pos-vel-acc)
## MLTleader(1)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('4.6')
## Problem1('22')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Applied Problems', 'Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Speed', 'Position')
DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(    'PGstandard.pl',      'PGML.pl',    'contextFraction.pl',     'PGcourse.pl'
);

#    s(t) = (1/A)t^2 - ln(t + B)
#    v(t) = (2/A)t - 1/(t + B)
#    a(t) = 2/A + 1/(t + B)^2

#    A and B are created as follows:
#      o  Generate times t1 and t2, where t1 > t2
#      o  A = 2 t1 t2, B = t1 - t2

#    For part c), this leads to the quadratic t^2 + (t1 - t2) - t1 t2 = 0 ==> (t + t1)(t - t2) = 0

#    For part d), the particle is speeding up over the interval (t2,inf)
Context("Numeric");

$T1 = random(3, 10);
$T2 = random(1, $T1 - 1);
$A  = Formula("2*$T1*$T2")->reduce;
$B  = Formula("$T1 - $T2")->reduce;
$C1 = Formula("1/$A")->reduce;
$C2 = Formula("2/$A")->reduce;
#   Use Fraction context for display variables

Context("Fraction")
    ->flags->set(reduceConstantFunctions => 0, reduceConstants => 0);
Context()->variables->add(t => 'Real');
$Pfuncfrac = Formula("(1/$A) t^2 - ln(t + $B)")->reduce;
$Vfuncfrac = Formula("(2/$A) t - 1/(t + $B)")->reduce;
$Afuncfrac = Formula("2/$A + 1/(t + $B)^2")->reduce;
$Vfrac     = Formula("2/$A")->reduce;
$tmp       = Formula("1 + $B")->reduce;

$Postsub     = Formula("1/$A (1)^2 - ln(1 + $B)");
$Postsubsimp = Formula("(1 - $A*ln($tmp))/$A");

$Veltsub     = Formula("(2/$A) (1) - 1/(1 + $B)");
$Veltsubsimp = Formula("2/$A - 1/$tmp")->reduce;
$Spdtsimp    = Compute("abs($Veltsubsimp)");

$Acctsub     = Formula("2/$A + 1/(1 + $B)^2");
$Acctsubsimp = Formula("2/$A + 1/$tmp^2")->reduce;

Context("Numeric")->variables->add(t => 'Real');
$Posfunc = Formula("(1/$A) t^2 - ln(t + $B)");
#   (a) Velocity & Acceleration functions
$Velfunc = Formula("(2/$A) t - 1/(t + $B)")->reduce;
$Accfunc = Formula("2/$A + 1/(t + $B)^2")->reduce;
#   (b) Position, velocity, speed & acceleration at t = 1
$Post = $Posfunc->eval(t => 1)->reduce;
$Velt = $Velfunc->eval(t => 1)->reduce;
$Acct = $Accfunc->eval(t => 1)->reduce;
$Spdt = Compute("abs($Velt)");
#   (c) Time when particle is stopped : Solve (2/A)t - 1/(t + B) = 0
$Stopped   = Formula("$T2");
$Quadratic = Formula("t^2 + $B t - $A/2")->reduce;
$Factors   = Formula("(t + $T1)(t - $T2)");
#   (d) Times when particle is speeding up/slowing down
#       -  Velocity and acceleration must be same sign
#       -  Acceleration is always positive here, so just want v(t) > 0 ==>  (t2,inf)
$Speedup    = Interval("($T2,inf)");
$Slowdown   = Interval("[0,$T2)");
$tmp        = $T1 - $T2;
$Inequality = Formula("(t + $T1)(t - $T2)/($T1 $T2(t + $tmp))")->reduce;


BEGIN_PGML
The function [` s(t) `] describes the position of a particle moving along a coordinate line,
where [` s `] is in feet and [` t `] is in seconds.
[``` s(t) = [$Posfunc], \qquad t \ge 0 ```]
If appropriate, enter answers in radical form. Use *inf* to represent [`\infty`].

a) Find the velocity and acceleration functions. 

    [# [. [`v(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Velfunc} .]*
     [. [`a(t)=`] .] [. [_]{$Accfunc} .]* 
     #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

b) Find the position, velocity, speed, and acceleration at [`t = [$B]`].

    [# [. Position ([`\textrm{ft}`]):   .] [. [_]{$Post} .]*
      [.  Velocity ([`\textrm{ft/ses}`]): .] [. [_]{$Velt} .]*
      [.  Speed ([`\textrm{ft/sec}`]):   .] [. [_]{$Spdt} .]*
      [.  Acceleration ([`\textrm{ft/sec}^2`]): .] [. [_]{$Acct} .]*
    #]{center => 0, align => 'rl', padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}

c) At what times is the particle stopped? Enter as a comma-separated list.

    [`t =`] [_]{$Stopped}


d) When is the particle speeding up? Slowing down? Enter using interval notation.

    [# [. Speeding Up:   .] [. [_]{$Speedup} .]*
      [.  Slowing Down: .] [. [_]{$Slowdown} .]*
    #]{center => 0, padding => [0.25, 0.1], valign => 'middle'}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) To find the velocity function, take the derivative of the position function, [` s(t)`]. 
    [``` s\;'(t) = v(t) = [$Vfuncfrac].```]
        To find the acceleration function, take the derivative of the velocity function.
    [``` v\;'(t) = a(t) = [$Afuncfrac].```]
        Note that this is also the second derivative of the position function.

b) To find the position, velocity, and acceleration at [`t = 1`], evaluate each of the
functions at this value.
    [``` \begin{aligned}
         s(1) &= [$Postsub] = [$Postsubsimp] \;\mbox{ft} \\
         v(1) &= [$Veltsub] = [$Veltsubsimp] \;\mbox{ft/sec} \\
         a(1) &= [$Acctsub] = [$Acctsubsimp] \;\mbox{ft/sec}^2\\
       \end{aligned}
    ```]
    To find the speed, use the absolute value of the velocity.
    [``` speed = |[$Veltsubsimp]| = [$Spdtsimp] \;\mbox{ft/sec}.```]

c) The particle is stopped whenever its velocity equals 0. So solve the equation [`v(t) = 0`].

    [``` [$Vfuncfrac] = 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$Quadratic] = 0 \quad \Rightarrow [$Factors] = 0.```]
    Discarding the negative root, we get [` t = [$T2]`] sec.

d) To determine when the particle is speeding up or slowing down, 
    we need to look at the signs of thevelocity and acceleration:

    - If the velocity is positive, then a positive acceleration 
will cause it to speed up
    - If the velocity is negative, then a negative acceleration
will cause it to speed up

    Therefore, the particle is speeding up whenever the velocity and acceleration are of the same sign. In this case, note that the acceleration function, [` a(t) = [$Afuncfrac]`], is positive for all [` t`].
So to determine when the particle is speeding up we need to find where the velocity is positive.
[``` \normalsize{[$Vfuncfrac]} > 0 \quad \Rightarrow \;\; [$Inequality] > 0```]
Solving this inequalities yields [` t > [$T2]`]. So the particle is speeding up over [` ([$T2],\infty)`].

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
